[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Project Structure and Configuration Files in NativeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding the layout of a NativeScript project and its configuration files is crucial for efficient mobile application development. Whether you are using TypeScript, JavaScript, Angular, or Vue, the fundamental skeleton remains consistent."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Project Directories"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you initialize a new NativeScript project, you are greeted with a well-defined folder structure. Here are the primary directories you will work with daily:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"app/ or src/: Contains the source code of your application, including views, view-models, styles, and assets."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"node_modules/: Houses all the project dependencies, including NativeScript core modules and third-party plugins."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"platforms/: Automatically generated by the CLI. Contains the native iOS and Android projects built from your source code."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Essential Configuration Files"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"NativeScript relies on several configuration files in the root directory to manage dependencies, build settings, and platform-specific properties."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"package.json: Manages project metadata, npm dependencies, and NativeScript-specific configurations like the runtimes."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"nsconfig.json: Configures NativeScript CLI options, such as custom app paths and webpack settings."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"tsconfig.json: Configures TypeScript compilation settings (if TypeScript is used)."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Examining package.json"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The package.json file is the heart of your project. Below is an example of a standard NativeScript configuration block inside package.json:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"{
  "name": "my-nativescript-app",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "main": "app.js",
  "dependencies": {
    "@nativescript/core": "~8.5.0",
    "@nativescript/theme": "~3.0.2"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@nativescript/types": "~8.5.0",
    "typescript": "~4.9.0"
  },
  "nativescript": {
    "id": "org.nativescript.myapp"
  }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Examining nsconfig.json"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"The nsconfig.json file allows you to customize the behavior of the NativeScript CLI. For instance, you can change the default app directory or point to a custom webpack configuration."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"{
  "appPath": "src",
  "appResourcesPath": "App_Resources",
  "webpackConfigPath": "webpack.config.js"
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Working with App_Resources"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The App_Resources folder contains platform-specific assets such as icons, splash screens, configuration manifests (AndroidManifest.xml), and Info.plist files for iOS. Modifications inside this folder directly dictate native application behaviors and metadata."}]
